{
  "description": "MAINTAINER RULING #984: a queued occurrence runs `once the handler is free` (spec/interaction-events.md:249-256), and is discarded ONLY once the main line has finished (:258-264). This fixture pins the boundary between those two clauses, and it exists because an implementation can satisfy the discard rule while still violating the run-it rule. Its twin `every-queued-occurrence-discarded-when-run-closes` is this same program WITHOUT the trailing `print`: there the main line ends after the `wait`, so the last queued occurrence is correctly discarded and the run prints twice. Here one more top-level statement follows, so the run is still open and the handler is free -- and that occurrence MUST run. Measured: a, a, a, main. The third `a` fires at the statement boundary before `print \"main\"`, and the occurrence its body queues in turn is discarded when the main line ends, so the count does not run away. A runtime that drains only inside the tick dispatch prints a, a, main -- it never notices the handler became free after its last tick, which is the rejected `drop the missed occurrence` reading reached by a different route. Draining once per boundary rather than looping is what keeps the two clauses compatible: an overrunning handler gets exactly one invocation per statement the main line still has.",
